{"documentId":"tos.work.sextus-empiricus.outlines-of-pyrrhonism","versionId":"retained-outlines-en-forage2026-ai","revision":"5796826c1e9b123b1253409ad3056e735c353f4f4bf94b1bdf84be7251587ff7","units":[{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-be02900ee36b4ee2bc477af73b0008ab","ordinal":201,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.201","text":"\n\nThe phrases “I am non-apprehensive” and “I do not apprehend” also indicate a feeling of one’s own, in which the skeptic refrains, for the present, from positing or rejecting any of the non-evident matters under investigation—as is clear from what we have said about the other phrases."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-270d55c8b0a847d4ba4fc1c81fe86378","ordinal":202,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.202","text":"\n\nWhen we say “to every argument an equal argument is opposed,” by “every” we mean every one we have examined. By “argument” we mean not argument in general, but argument that establishes something dogmatically, that is, about something non-evident—and not necessarily one made up of premises and a conclusion, but one that establishes in any way whatever. By “equal” we mean equal in credibility or lack of credibility; we take “is opposed” in the general sense of “conflicts”; and we supply “as it appears to me.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-db91e3ef418c49049b716121506249fc","ordinal":203,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.203","text":"\n\nSo when I say “to every argument an equal argument is opposed,” I am in effect saying: “To every argument investigated by me which establishes something dogmatically, there appears to me to be opposed another argument, establishing something dogmatically, equal to it in credibility and lack of credibility.” So uttering the phrase is not dogmatic; it is the report of a human feeling, which is apparent to the person who feels it."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5787f78c173c4103b9c8512cc811fe26","ordinal":204,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.204","text":"\n\nSome also utter the phrase in this form: “to every argument an equal argument is to be opposed,” meaning it as an instruction: “To every argument that establishes something dogmatically, let us oppose an argument that investigates dogmatically, equal in credibility and lack of credibility, and conflicting with it.” For them the formula is addressed to the skeptic, and they use the infinitive in place of the imperative—“to be opposed” in place of “let us oppose.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b6620fd68c504b4f90fc37748c6531a3","ordinal":205,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.205","text":"\n\nThey give this instruction to the skeptic in case he is somehow tricked by the dogmatist into giving up the investigation of it, and through rashness misses the tranquility that appears to them—the tranquility they think follows on suspension of judgment about everything, as we pointed out earlier."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-344b5109c4e341efac69a45f892e4b36","ordinal":206,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.206","text":"\n\nIt will be enough to have gone through this many of the phrases, as befits an outline, especially since what we have now said makes it possible to speak about those we have left out as well. About all the skeptical phrases we must grasp in advance that we do not affirm that they are definitely true—after all, we say that they can be done away with by themselves, being cancelled along with the things they are said about, just as purgative drugs not only drive the humors out of the body but flush themselves out along with the humors."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7e4fd08ed2994eb38e26e7baf54f0f28","ordinal":207,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.207","text":"\n\nWe also say that we use them not as indicating strictly the things they are applied to, but indifferently, and loosely if people like. For it does not suit the skeptic to fight about words; and besides, it works in our favor that not even these phrases are said to signify purely, but relatively—relative, that is, to the skeptics."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-5abb7ff85fbd41708583d19daf85367b","ordinal":208,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.208","text":"\n\nIn addition, we must remember that we do not say them about all things universally, but about what is non-evident and investigated dogmatically; and that we say what appears to us, and make no affirmative declarations about the nature of external objects. For on these grounds, I think, every sophism brought against a skeptical phrase can be overturned."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-c4d9a55ea9e943f6a4ebe746e722da46","ordinal":209,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.209","text":"\n\nWe have now made clear the character of skepticism by going through its concept, its parts, its criterion and its goal, and also the modes of suspension, and by discussing the skeptical phrases. So we think it fitting to go briefly through the distinction between skepticism and the philosophies close to it, so that we may understand the suspensive way more clearly. Let us begin with the philosophy of Heraclitus."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-56e1bfd729984018867369b9c6c2cf76","ordinal":210,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.210","text":"\n\nThat this philosophy differs from our way is evident: Heraclitus makes dogmatic declarations about many non-evident things, and we do not, as has been said. But Aenesidemus and his followers said that the skeptical way is a road to the Heraclitean philosophy. Their reason was that opposites’ appearing to hold of the same thing comes before their actually holding of it; and the skeptics say that opposites appear to hold of the same thing, while the Heracliteans move on from this to their actually holding. Against them we say that opposites’ appearing to hold of the same thing is not a belief of the skeptics but a fact that strikes not only the skeptics but other philosophers and all human beings too.\n〔1〕 Heraclitus of Ephesus (c. 500 BC) taught that opposites coexist in the same thing: “the way up and the way down are one and the same.”"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b360295237f84a798352ea54aa8cafdd","ordinal":211,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.211","text":"\n\nNo one, at any rate, would dare to say that honey does not taste sweet to the healthy, or that it does not taste bitter to people with jaundice. So the Heracliteans start from a preconception common to all human beings, just as we do—and perhaps the other philosophies do too. If, then, they took the claim that opposites hold of the same thing from one of the things skeptics say, such as “everything is inapprehensible” or “I determine nothing” or something similar, perhaps they would draw the conclusion they claim. But since their starting points strike not only us but other philosophers and ordinary life as well, why should anyone say that our way, rather than each of the other philosophies, or ordinary life itself, is a road to the Heraclitean philosophy, since we all make use of common material?"},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b59a509e0d8f4882b70345445e253417","ordinal":212,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.212","text":"\n\nPerhaps the skeptical way not only does not help toward knowing the Heraclitean philosophy but actually hinders it. For the skeptic disparages all of Heraclitus’ beliefs as rashly stated: he opposes the conflagration, he opposes the claim that opposites hold of the same thing, and in the case of every belief of Heraclitus he mocks the dogmatic rashness and says “I do not apprehend” and “I determine nothing,” as I said earlier—which conflicts with the Heracliteans. But it is absurd to say that a conflicting way is a road to the very school it conflicts with. It is therefore absurd to say that the skeptical way is a road to the Heraclitean philosophy.\n〔1〕 The “conflagration” is the periodic destruction of the cosmos by fire, which the Stoics attributed to Heraclitus."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-6abc3c66ff5d4d91837261d4fe90851a","ordinal":213,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.213","text":"\n\nThe philosophy of Democritus, too, is said to have something in common with skepticism, since it seems to use the same material as we do. For from the fact that honey appears sweet to some and bitter to others, Democritus, they say, reasons that it is neither sweet nor bitter, and for this reason utters the phrase “no more,” which is a skeptical phrase. But the skeptics and the Democriteans use the phrase “no more” differently. The Democriteans apply it to neither alternative being the case; we apply it to our not knowing whether both, or neither, of the things that appear is the case."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ccb3c2aada2a4fa290d736b7b91f934e","ordinal":214,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.214","text":"\n\nSo we differ in this respect too; but the distinction becomes most evident when Democritus says “in reality, atoms and void.” By “in reality” he means “in truth”; and since he says that the atoms and the void exist in truth, it is superfluous, I think, to say that he differs from us, even if he starts from the variation in what appears."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d5ff08fb109345d9ba46e445dc8ee84b","ordinal":215,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.215","text":"\n\nSome say that the Cyrenaic way is the same as skepticism, since it too says that only feelings are apprehended. But it differs from skepticism. It says that the goal is pleasure and the smooth motion of the flesh, whereas we say it is tranquility, to which their goal is opposed: for whether pleasure is present or not, anyone who affirms that pleasure is the goal suffers disturbances, as I argued in the section on the goal. Then, too, we suspend judgment about external objects, as far as the argument goes, whereas the Cyrenaics declare that they have an inapprehensible nature."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-94f6e9bf25694c15bd929bfbdebbe2a6","ordinal":216,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.216","text":"\n\nProtagoras, too, holds that a human being is the measure of all things—of things that are, that they are, and of things that are not, that they are not. By “measure” he means the criterion, and by “things” he means objects; so that in effect he is saying that a human being is the criterion of all objects—of things that are, that they are, and of things that are not, that they are not. And for this reason he posits only what appears to each person, and in this way introduces relativity.\n〔1〕 Protagoras of Abdera was a fifth-century BC sophist; his “a human being is the measure” opened his book Truth."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7d57f26397e64edc8b5dcab399e80025","ordinal":217,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.217","text":"\n\nThat is why he seems to have something in common with the Pyrrhonians. But he differs from them, and we will see the difference once we have set out, at moderate length, what Protagoras thought. He says that matter is in flux, and that as it flows, additions continually arise to replace what is given off; and that the senses are rearranged and altered depending on ⟨their⟩ ages and on the other constitutions of bodies."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-4fc99123e09c41eca989705f835d61cd","ordinal":218,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.218","text":"\n\nHe also says that the principles of everything that appears are present in matter, so that matter, as far as it itself goes, can be everything that appears to everyone. Human beings, he says, perceive different things at different times depending on their different conditions: someone in a natural state apprehends those things in matter which can appear to people in a natural state, and someone in an unnatural state those which can appear to people in an unnatural state."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b2c1854c3fd745f592250b8d9f2cc1a5","ordinal":219,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.219","text":"\n\nAnd again, the same account holds depending on ages, on being asleep or awake, and on every kind of condition. So according to him the human being becomes the criterion of what exists: for everything that appears to human beings also is, and what appears to no human being is not. We see, then, that he holds beliefs both about matter being in flux and about the principles of everything that appears being present in it—things that are non-evident, and on which we suspend judgment."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b07001e07df84d5fabf3b1007cb3493b","ordinal":220,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.220","text":"\n\nSome people, however, say that the Academic philosophy is the same as skepticism, so it would be fitting to go through this question too. According to ⟨most⟩ accounts there have been three Academies. The first and most ancient is that of Plato and his circle. The second, or Middle, Academy is that of Arcesilaus, the pupil of Polemo. The third, or New, Academy is that of Carneades and Clitomachus. Some add a fourth as well, that of Philo and Charmidas, and some even count a fifth, that of Antiochus.\n〔1〕 Arcesilaus (third century BC) first turned the Academy skeptical; Philo of Larissa and Antiochus of Ascalon (first century BC), its last heads, moved it back toward doctrine."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-cb24aaf7ffb642e79cde9e23d1dba01b","ordinal":221,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.221","text":"\n\nLet us begin with the ancient one, then, and see how the philosophies just mentioned differ ⟨from us⟩. Some have said that Plato was dogmatic, others that he was aporetic, and others that he was aporetic in some respects and dogmatic in others. In his exercise dialogues [they say], where Socrates is brought on either playing with certain people or contending against sophists, his character is exercising and aporetic. But it is dogmatic where he speaks seriously and makes pronouncements, whether through Socrates or Timaeus or someone of that kind."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e13aa33ba41942f5bc1d87c70fe4ba19","ordinal":222,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.222","text":"\n\nNow, about those who say he is dogmatic, or dogmatic in some respects and aporetic in others, it would be superfluous to speak at present, for they themselves admit that he differs from us. Whether he is purely skeptical we discuss more fully in our commentaries. For now, as in an outline, we say, following ⟨the circle of⟩ Menodotus and Aenesidemus (they were the chief champions of this position), that when Plato makes pronouncements about forms, or about there being providence, or about the life of excellence being more choiceworthy than the life of vice, there are two possibilities. If he assents to these things as real, he holds beliefs. If he accepts them as more persuasive, then, since he prefers one thing to another on grounds of credibility or lack of credibility, he has abandoned the skeptical character. That this too is foreign to us is evident from what we said earlier.\n〔1〕 Menodotus of Nicomedia (second century AD) was a doctor of the Empiric school and a leading skeptic."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-0d40596c2b574d619c897abd3c10e69b","ordinal":223,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.223","text":"\n\nEven if he puts forward some things in a skeptical way when, as they say, he is exercising, that will not make him a skeptic. For someone who holds a belief about even one thing, or who prefers one impression to another at all on grounds of credibility or lack of credibility, ⟨or makes assertions⟩ about anything non-evident, takes on the dogmatic character. Timon too shows this by what he says about Xenophanes."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-b89df36309334ad69450120f63aff411","ordinal":224,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.224","text":"\n\nTimon praised him [Xenophanes] in many respects, so far as even to dedicate his Lampoons to him, and he made him lament and say: “If only I too had met with a shrewd mind,\none that looks both ways! But I was cheated by a treacherous road,\nold as I already was, and heedless of all\nskepticism. For wherever I turned my mind,\neverything resolved into one and the same; and all that is, forever\ndrawn back every way, settled into a single, uniform nature.” That, at any rate, is why he calls him “half free of conceit,” and not perfectly free of conceit, in the lines: “Xenophanes, half free of conceit, mocker of Homer’s deceit,\nwho fashioned a god far from human beings, equal on every side,\n⟨unshaken⟩, unharmed, more mind-like than thought.” He called him “half free of conceit” because he was free of conceit in some respect, and “mocker of Homer’s deceit” because he ridiculed the deceit in Homer.\n〔1〕 Timon of Phlius (third century BC), Pyrrho’s disciple, wrote satirical poems called Silloi (“Lampoons”). Xenophanes of Colophon (sixth century BC) was a poet-philosopher who mocked Homer’s gods."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-37e94c06708543388d67c2c94733325f","ordinal":225,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.225","text":"\n\nXenophanes held the belief, against the preconceptions of other people, that the universe is one, and that god is grown together with all things and is spherical, impassive, unchangeable and rational. So it is easy to show how Xenophanes differs from us. In any case, it is evident from what has been said that even if Plato raises puzzles about some things, he would not be a skeptic. For in some places he appears either to make pronouncements about the reality of non-evident things or to prefer some non-evident things to others on grounds of credibility."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e3b242e709aa488e8891cd80882376c4","ordinal":226,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.226","text":"\n\nThe members of the New Academy say that everything is inapprehensible. Even so, they differ from the skeptics, perhaps precisely in saying that everything is inapprehensible. They affirm this firmly, whereas the skeptic expects that it is possible for some things to be apprehended as well. But they evidently differ from us in their judgment of good and bad things. When the Academics say that something is good or bad, they do not say it as we do. They say it with the conviction that what they call good is more persuasively good than the opposite, and likewise with the bad. When we say that something is good or bad, we do not think that what we say is persuasive. We simply follow life without holding opinions, so as not to be inactive."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f85b1bd22c954ca39f1b5f418dc0b9f7","ordinal":227,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.227","text":"\n\nWe say that impressions are equal in credibility or lack of credibility, as far as the argument goes. They say that some impressions are persuasive and others unpersuasive. They also distinguish among persuasive impressions. Some, they think, are merely persuasive. Others are persuasive and examined. Still others are persuasive, tested and undiverted. For instance, when a coiled rope is lying in a fairly dark room, someone who rushes in all at once gets a simply persuasive impression from it, as if it came from a snake."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-53e6c78809744168ac328e917ad3a465","ordinal":228,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.228","text":"\n\nBut someone who has looked around carefully and examined the details may notice that it does not move, that its color is such-and-such, and each of the other points. To him it appears to be a rope, in virtue of the impression that is persuasive and tested. The undiverted impression is like this. Heracles is said to have brought Alcestis back up from Hades after she had died, and to have shown her to Admetus. Admetus received an impression of Alcestis that was persuasive and tested. But since he knew that she had died, his mind was diverted from assent and inclined toward disbelief. ⟨....⟩\n〔1〕 In the myth, dramatized in Euripides’ Alcestis, Alcestis died in place of her husband Admetus, and Heracles won her back from Death."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-bf847a188fcf464db5ca66234e36271c","ordinal":229,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.229","text":"\n\nSo the members of the New Academy prefer the persuasive and tested impression to the simply persuasive one. To both of these they prefer the impression that is persuasive, tested and undiverted. And even if both the Academics and the skeptics say that they are persuaded by certain things, the difference between the philosophies is evident on this point too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ad721b1899c14eddb9e0d9ee3ac0a0a7","ordinal":230,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.230","text":"\n\n“Being persuaded” is said in different senses. In one sense it means not resisting but simply going along, without strong inclination or attachment, as a child is said to be persuaded by (that is, to obey) his attendant. In another sense it means assenting to something by choice and with a kind of sympathy, out of a strong desire, as a profligate is persuaded by someone who urges him to live extravagantly. Carneades, Clitomachus and their followers say, with strong inclination, that they are persuaded and that something is persuasive. We say so in the sense of simply yielding, without attachment. So we would differ from them on this point too."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-f7feee96557c4470871f9eef1557b596","ordinal":231,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.231","text":"\n\nWe also differ from the New Academy in matters bearing on the goal of life. The men who say they order their lives by its rule make use of the persuasive in living. We live without holding opinions, following laws, customs and natural feelings. And we would have said more to draw the distinction if we were not aiming at brevity."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-7124cf241ce14eb997053bf53a7446fd","ordinal":232,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.232","text":"\n\nArcesilaus, however, whom we called the head and founder of the Middle Academy, seems to me to share a great deal in the Pyrrhonian arguments, so that his way and ours are practically one. He is not found making pronouncements about the reality or unreality of anything, and he does not prefer one thing to another on grounds of credibility or lack of credibility. He suspends judgment about everything. He says that the goal is suspension of judgment, which, as we said, is accompanied by tranquility."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-8a4b95769f914057bfdaf2e3615d3d0d","ordinal":233,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.233","text":"\n\nHe also says that particular suspensions of judgment are good and particular assents are bad. One might object that we say these things according to what appears to us and not affirmatively, whereas he says them as statements about the nature of things. On that view he would actually be saying that suspension of judgment is good and assent is bad."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e68cf4741b504885b6c0d0749e16af7c","ordinal":234,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.234","text":"\n\nIf we are also to trust what is said about him, they say that on the surface he appeared to be a Pyrrhonian, but in truth he was a dogmatist. He used aporetic argument to test his companions, to see whether they had the natural gifts to take up Plato’s beliefs. For this reason he was thought to be aporetic, though he would pass on Plato’s doctrines to those companions who were gifted. That is why Ariston said of him: “Plato in front, Pyrrho behind, Diodorus in the middle.” He made use of Diodorus’ dialectic, but he was an out-and-out Platonist.\n〔1〕 Ariston of Chios was a Stoic. His line parodies Homer’s Chimaera (“lion in front, serpent behind, goat in the middle”), and Diodorus Cronus was a dialectician famous for logical puzzles."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-32aeb797b32f40ca8f85bce0930b06f6","ordinal":235,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.235","text":"\n\nPhilo and his followers say that things are inapprehensible as far as the Stoic criterion goes, that is, the apprehensive impression. As far as the nature of the things themselves goes, however, they say things are apprehensible. Antiochus, for his part, transferred the Stoa into the Academy, so that it was even said of him that he did Stoic philosophy in the Academy. He tried to show that the Stoics’ beliefs are already to be found in Plato. So the difference between the skeptical way and the so-called Fourth and Fifth Academies is evident."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-89f4082c141048eeb39fb9699069404f","ordinal":236,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.236","text":"\n\nSome people also say that the skeptical philosophy is the same as the Empiric school of medicine. It should be understood that if that Empiricism firmly affirms that non-evident things are inapprehensible, then it is not the same as skepticism, and it would not suit the skeptic to take up that school. It seems to me that he could rather follow the so-called Method.\n〔1〕 Ancient medicine had rival schools. The Rationalists inferred hidden causes, the Empirics relied on experience, and the Methodists treated observable “commonalities” of disease."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-d22201c3e71c46ed91c5d938f2462b1b","ordinal":237,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.237","text":"\n\nOf all the medical schools, this one alone seems not to be rash about non-evident things, presuming to say whether they are apprehensible or inapprehensible. It follows appearances and takes from them what seems beneficial, in keeping with the skeptics’ practice. We said earlier that the common life, which the skeptic also makes use of, has four parts. One lies in the guidance of nature, one in the compulsion of feelings, one in the handing down of laws and customs, and one in the teaching of arts."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-dda3dd4a1ec8467a99707de9d0eb5b7c","ordinal":238,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.238","text":"\n\nBy the compulsion of feelings, the skeptic is led to drink by thirst, to food by hunger, and likewise to other things. In the same way, the Methodist doctor is led by the affections to what corresponds to them. Constriction leads him to relaxation, just as someone takes refuge in warmth from the contraction caused by intense cold. Flux leads him to stop it, just as people in a bathhouse who are dripping with sweat and growing faint come to stop it, and for that reason take refuge in the cool air. It is also evident that things alien to nature compel us to set about removing them, since even a dog, when a thorn has stuck in it, sets about getting it out."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-e114a0762f9e48fc8ebcb5d2acf38d95","ordinal":239,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.239","text":"\n\nI do not want to depart from the outline character of this treatise by speaking about each case. So I will say only that I think everything the Methodists say in this way can be brought under the compulsion of the affections, both those in accordance with nature ⟨and those contrary to nature⟩. Besides, both ways have in common an undogmatic and indifferent use of words."},{"id":"tos.text-unit.sid-ed7eb9a50e414a52bd48f6c6a5fbf41e","ordinal":240,"kind":"section","language":"en","reference":"Book 1 · §1.240","text":"\n\nThe skeptic uses the phrases “I determine nothing” and “I apprehend nothing” ⟨without holding opinions⟩, as we have said. In the same way the Methodist speaks of “commonality” and “pervading” and the like, without overelaboration. He also takes up the term “indication” without holding opinions, meaning by it the guidance from the apparent affections, both natural and unnatural, toward what seem to be the corresponding remedies. I mentioned this in the case of thirst, hunger and the rest."}]}